<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ha Long Bay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ha Long Bay is a bay in the Gulf of Tonkin in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, known for its thousands of limestone islands and pillars. On 5 June 1948 an agreement signed aboard a French warship in the bay recognised in principle the independence of Vietnam within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a>, preparing the return of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bảo Đại</a>. The bay has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ha-long-bay.jpg" alt="Ha Long Bay" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Taewangkorea · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ha_Long_Bay_in_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
